    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d406">

                <pb facs="0859" n="818" sameAs="frus:frus1949v03/pg818"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 Council/5–2249: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Secretary of State
                        at Paris</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d406-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">us urgent</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-05-26T20:00:00-04:00">May 26, 1949—8 p.
                            m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p> Secdel 1610. Personal for the Secretary from Webb. The Joint Chiefs of Staff
                    have considered your alternative number three of <pb facs="0860" n="819"
                        xml:id="pg819"/> Delsec 1784<anchor n="2" xml:id="d406-f2"/> as a separate
                    problem from overall study you suggested in order to provide you with an early
                    answer. Begin quote of JCS views:</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>“With reference to alternative number three as contained in Delsec 1784 of 22
                        May 1949, the Joint Chiefs of Staff believe there would be nothing gained by
                        such a plan. In the first place any reinstitution of the Berlin blockade
                        would now in the view of the Joint Chiefs of Staff be dangerously close to
                        an act of war, and, if the “probing” were then to follow there would be
                        likelihood that the incidence of the war would be laid at the door of the
                        United States. Consequently, they believe that by so-called “probing” much
                        would be risked extending from serious loss of prestige to the distinct
                        possibility of involvement in hostilities even though they assume that the
                        plan is not intended to include the use of force. The Joint Chiefs of Staff
                        are considering the whole problem of possible U.S. courses of action in the
                        event the USSR reimposes the Berlin blockade and their views will be
                        available for forwarding to you at an early date.”<anchor n="3"
                            xml:id="d406-f3"/>
                    </p>
                </quote>
                <p>The Dept supports the above views of the JCS on the isolated issue of probing
                    tactics. Our general feeling is that half-way measures in a matter of this type
                    with the Soviets are likely to be unsuccessful and are, in some respects, even
                    more dangerous than more deliberate courses of action.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Webb</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d406-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d406-f1-content">
                <p>Mr. Acheson was attending a meeting of the Council of Foreign Ministers, at
                    Paris. For documentation, see <ref target="#pg913">pp. 913</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d406-f2" n="2" xml:id="d406-f2-content">
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Supra.</hi>
                </p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d406-f3" n="3" xml:id="d406-f3-content">
                <p>For the text of the Joint Chiefs of Staff study on the courses of action in the
                    event of the reimposition of the Berlin blockade by the Soviet Union, see NSC
                    24/2, June 1, Appendix A, <ref target="#pg821">p. 821</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
